==English==
{{was wotd|2006|July|18}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|pandar}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|pandare}}, from Chaucer’s character {{w|Pandarus|Pandare}} (in ''{{w|Troilus and Criseyde}}''; see also Shakespeare’s ''{{w|Troilus and Cressida}}''), from {{der|en|it|Pandaro}} (found in {{w|Boccaccio}}), from {{der|en|la|Pandarus}} (found in {{w|Greek mythology}}), from {{der|en|grc|Πάνδαρος}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈpændə/}}
* {{a|GA}} {{IPA|en|/ˈpændɚ/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-pander.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-pander.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ændə(ɹ)|s=2}}
* {{a|non-rhotic accents}} {{homophones|en|panda}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A person who furthers the [[illicit]] love-affairs of others; a [[pimp]] or [[procurer]].
#: {{synonyms|en|panderer|Thesaurus:pimp}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1609|passage={{...}} if ever you prove false one, to another since I have taken such paine to bring you together let all pittifull goers betweene be cald to the worlds end after my name, call them all Panders, let all constant men be Troylusses all false woemen Cressids, and all brokers betweene '''panders'''|author=Shakespeare|title=Troilus and Cressida|section=Act 3}}
#* '''1992''', Moncrieff/Kilmartin/Enright, translating Marcel Proust, ''Swann’s Way'', Folio Society 2005, p. 190:
#*: It was not only the brilliant phalanx of virtuous dowagers, generals and academicians with whom he was most intimately associated that Swann so cynically compelled to serve him as '''panders'''.
# An offer of illicit [[sex]] with a third party.
# An illicit or illegal [[offer]], usually to [[tempt]].
# {{lb|en|by extension}} One who ministers to the evil designs and passions of another.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Winter's Tale|II|ii|282|1|''Camillo'' was his helpe in this, his '''Pandar''': <br>There is a Plot against my Life, my Crowne; <br>All's true that is mistrusted: that false Villaine, <br>Whom I employ'd, was pre-emplot'd}} <!--- probably Leontes is (unconsciously) punning on pandar ("pimp"), but the literal meaning is as the sense --->
#* {{RQ:Burke Noble Lord|passage=Those wicked '''panders''' to avarice and ambition.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/magazine/archive/1944/05/chanute-and-points-west/656600/|title=Chanute and Points West|year=1944|month=May|author=Gilbert E. Fuller Jr|work=The Atlantic|passage=Two small Hindu boys were waiting for me outside—small for their years (about fourteen) but infinitely worldly-wise. Hindu boys are, first, '''panders''', and second, remarkably skillful and pertinacious beggars.}}

